 \section{Conclusion and future directions}
 \label{sec:conclusion}
 
-We characterized the stationary mean bias of \Scaf{} and matched \FedAvg{} under a common homogeneous stochastic model. Both algorithms share a leading $\gamma/N$ contribution, but their client-independent $\gamma^2$ coefficients differ. The matched comparison identifies the additional contribution of persistent SCAFFOLD controls and traces it to changes in within-round local second moments that interact with third-order curvature. Our uniform remainder bounds allow these coefficients to be identified along joint small-step and large-client limits. An exact control translation extends the SCAFFOLD characterization to affine objective shifts. Controlled experiments examine coefficient and local-step predictions, while real-data experiments show how fixed-budget minibatch allocation affects finite-tail parameter displacement.
+We derived explicit stationary mean-bias coefficients for \Scaf{} and matched
+\FedAvg{} under a common homogeneous stochastic model. Their shared
+$\gamma/N$ term and distinct client-independent $\gamma^2$ coefficients separate
+ordinary local-update and persistent-control contributions. The latter arises
+through local second moments coupled with third-order curvature. A uniform
+remainder identifies these coefficients along arbitrary joint small-step and
+large-client limits, and an exact control translation extends the SCAFFOLD
+result to affine objective shifts. Controlled experiments examine coefficient
+predictions; real-data studies show how minibatch allocation and temporal
+averaging change finite-tail parameter-error composition without establishing
+predictive harm.
 
-An important next step is to investigate how unequal client curvature and partial participation affect the coefficient decomposition, particularly because control variates are designed to address client drift. State-dependent gradient noise may introduce further interactions between the controls and local fluctuations. The explicit control-associated coefficient also suggests studying whether alternative control updates can reduce stationary bias while preserving their convergence benefits, and how the stationary characterization relates to finite-time bias and variance.+Future work can examine unequal client curvature, partial participation, and
+state-dependent gradient noise. The explicit control-associated coefficient
+also motivates studying bias-reducing control updates together with their
+finite-time convergence, variance, and computational cost.
